00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:04.390 align:start position:0%
 
hi  i'm khush madhya and i will be  presenting our work on agnostic learning  with unknown utilities  this is joint work with peter bartlett  ankar dragan and jacob steinhardt  in classical decision problems one often  begins with a data set  consisting of n data points these  consist of feature or context vectors x  i  and corresponding labels or decisions y  i the central objective in most  scenarios is to learn a classifier  to predict the labels y correctly given  context vectors  x within this paradigm  most works if not all implicitly assume  that all mistakes are equally costly and  that each scenario x  is equally important in reality though  this is often not the case for instance  consider the classification system  designed for a self-driving car  the cost of mispredicting a roadside  post of box  versus that of missing out on a stock  sign can actually be very different  furthermore consider the two scenarios  shown in the picture  one where a person is crossing the road  and the other where the person is  walking  far away from the roadside clearly  identifying the person correctly  is far more important in the first case  as compared to the second one  in our work we posit that each decision  taken by the learner  has a context dependent utility or a  negative cost  furthermore key characteristics of this  utility function  are that a it is unknown to the learner  a priori  before the task begins and b that it is  necessary for the human to communicate  it to the learner in some form  given this unknown utility the question  here is how do we communicate this  function to the learner  the first option is to possibly get a  human expert to write down this complex  utility function mathematically  and hand it over to the learner however  this option can be prone to serious  errors  since it is very hard for an expert to  imagine all possible scenarios a priori  and encode them in the utility function  another option is to allow the agent to  learn this utility function from  observed decisions  such a paradigm has been studied in the  economics literature  as learning from revealed preferences  and in the machine learning community  as inverse reinforcement learning the  main idea here  is to allow the learner to infer the  underlying utility function  by exhibiting optimal decision making in  different scenarios  however most of the techniques studied  here assume that this utility function  lies in some well-specified class of  functions say  linear in some feature space and it  offers no guarantees on their  performance  in the face of slight misspecification  hence both these options are not  actually suitable to communicate this  unknown utility function  in order to overcome the limitations of  these existing options  we introduce our framework of agnostic  learning with unknown utilities  wherein we place no assumptions on the  underlying utility function  you start formally  the learner is presented with a data set  of n points  each of which is sample iid from some  unknown distribution d  we denote by script f the decision  function class  wherein each function f is a mapping  from the context space  x to decision space y further  we denote by you start the true utility  function  which is again unknown to the learner  this utility function maps contexts and  corresponding decisions to rewards in a  bounded interval  lastly there is an information oracle  with which the learner can interact and  get more information  about the different decisions why and  the underlying utility you start  as i mentioned earlier our framework  moves away from placing any assumption  on the underlying utility function u  star  and instead posits that the decision  making function can only be selected  from this restricted class  f given this framework  the objective of the learner is to  output a function f hat  such that it has small excess risk when  evaluated on this unknown utility  function u star  observe that this is just an evaluation  criteria and that  true utility is never revealed to the  learner  with this setup the key question here is  what should be this information oracle  we would ideally like this article to be  powerful enough to make learning  feasible  but it should not be overly complicated  for human experts to answer the queries  before we discuss this issue in detail i  wanted to mention that  going forward we will restrict our  attention to the binary classification  problem  where the label space y consists of two  labels a blue label and a green label  coming back to the information oracles  the first contender for such an article  is the value oracle  in this article given a context x and a  decision  the human expert provides the exact  value of the underlying utility function  however note that the underlying utility  function can be quite complex  and this actually makes it very hard for  human experts to accurately provide  these value judgments  this actually makes it infeasible to  deploy such an oracle in practice  on the other hand it is often easier for  humans to provide comparative evaluation  based on these utilities  for instance one can query the human  with a context and a pair of decisions  and it can respond by indicating which  of these two  has a higher utility we call this the  comparison oracle  such comparative feedback however is  severely limited  in the information it provides to the  learner  for instance consider the problem where  we have three clusters of data points  x1 x2 and x3 the color of the data point  here  indicates which of the two decisions has  a higher utility  for instance for the cluster x1 the  green decision has a higher utility than  the blue one  the shaded areas mark the predicted  decisions for the two classifiers f1  and f2 observe that both classifiers f1  and f2  make the same number of mistakes f1  predict mispredicts  x3 while f2 mispredicts x1  also just by interacting with the  comparison oracle  no learner will be able to understand  the relative costs of these mistakes  indeed no learner can understand the  precise tradeoffs between the different  mistakes  and one can actually show a strong  statement which is that any algorithm  will suffer an excess risk which will be  order one  thus this comparison oracle while being  easy to implement on the human side  is actually information theoretically  limited  we can go to the other extreme and one  can imagine an extremely powerful oracle  which we call the infinite comparison  oracle  this oracle takes in two functions f1  and f2  and outputs which one of those has a  higher utility  when evaluated on the true distribution  d  note that this article can actually be  used by a learning algorithm to figure  out the best decision function in the  class f  however it places all the burden of  learning on the human expert  asking them to effectively compare  decisions across an infinite number of  contexts  thus while being information  theoretically optimal this article  is practically infeasible to implement  to overcome these limitations of  existing elicitation mechanisms  our work proposes a new type of  comparison oracle  which we call the k comparison oracle  this type of information feedback  interpolates between the vanilla and the  infinite comparison  it gives the human expert a bucket of k  different contexts  x1 to xk and two sets of associated  decision vectors  the human is then required to compare  these two bundles  and output which of these two has a  higher combined utility  note that one can recover the vanilla  and the infinite comparison oracles by  setting k equals to 1  and k equals to infinity respectively  it's import it's also important to note  here that as the order k of the  comparison oracle increases  it gets harder and harder for the human  to provide this feedback  going forward we'll focus on our problem  of agnostic learning with unknown  utilities  where the information oracle is the key  comparison oracle  we will describe our main algorithm  which interacts with the k comparison  oracle  to output a function f hat and we'll  also study its statistical properties  let's begin with our main algorithm in  this figure  the x-axis represents the different data  points  the color of each data point again  represents the decision with the higher  utility  the y-axis represents the utility gain  that is  how much additional utility can a  classifier gain by predicting green  versus blue for any particular data  point  further for illustration purposes we'll  restrict  f to be the class of threshold functions  and each function f  will therefore partition the space into  a blue and a green region  let's begin with a slightly easier  problem where the underlying utility  function u star  is actually known to the learner in this  case  the learner can simply output the  function f which maximizes the utility  on  n data points and this is also called  the empirical utility maximizer  this function f erm is known to have  good generalization properties  depending on the class of functions f  however since in our problem the utility  function u star is unknown  the learner cannot output the erm  function  instead our algorithm consists of a  two-step plug-in estimator  in the first step this estimator obtains  estimates you had  of the utility on the sample data points  by interacting with the k  comparison oracles the vertical bars on  the data points  reflect this estimation error  in the next step the learner simply  outputs the empirical maximizer with  respect to these estimated utilities  in the plot this is shown by the  threshold function  f hat alg given this procedure  i will now explain in detail how the  first step  that of estimating the utilities works  to obtain estimates you had on the  sample data points  the algorithm begins by finding the data  point x max  which has the maximum utility gain  this data point is highlighted in the  figure on the right  notice that this can actually be  obtained by simply using two comparison  queries  for instance to compare the gains of the  data points x1 and x2  we can ask a query comparing both  blue decisions for x1 and x2 with both  green decisions  this query effectively provides a  comparison between the utility gains of  x1  and x2 once we have the ability to  compare two data points  we can find the one which has the  maximum utility gain  once we have the point x max with us the  algorithm  can use this to estimate the utility  gains of each data point using k  comparisons  in order to see how consider the point x  prime  in the figure on the right for some  value of j  between 1 and k over 2 we can issue the  following k  query to the comparison oracle in the  query  we first set the first k by 2 elements  to be  points x prime and the next j elements  to be x  max such  a k query can be shown to be equivalent  to asking the human expert  whether k by 2 times the gain for x  prime is bigger  or j times the gain for x max  combining this with a binary search and  varying the values of j appropriately  one can actually obtain a 2 by k  approximate estimate of the utility gain  of x  prime similarly by iterating over  all the data points one can obtain the  estimates over the entire data set  and with this we complete the procedure  for obtaining these estimates  so just to recap our main algorithm is a  two-step plug-in estimator  in which the first step comprises  estimating utilities you had  on the sample data points by interacting  with the key comparison oracle  in the next step the algorithm outputs  the empirical utility maximizer with  respect to these estimated utilities  given this algorithm  the natural question to ask is what  about the excise risk of this estimator  which was our original objective to  begin with  our main upper bound theorem precisely  answers this question  in particular it says that the plugin  estimator  makes order n log k queries to the k  comparison oracle  and then its excess risk is upper  bounded by a sum of two terms  the first term which is a complexity  term captures the expressiveness of the  function class f  this depends on how the utility u star  interacts with the class f but more  importantly  it places no restrictions whatsoever on  this utility function u star  more formally this term captures the  rate of uniform convergence of the class  f when evaluated on the utility u star  the second term is a product of two  terms  the first is a factor of the order of  one over k  and the next one is the misprediction  error  of the optimal erm function  observe that in the well specified case  when the ern correctly predicts the  labels of each data point  this term vanishes and one does not pay  any additional error  for not knowing the utility function  thus the consequences of not knowing the  utility function  only come up in the misspecified setup  further note that this theorem provides  an example of an interesting  accuracy elicitation tradeoff  as the order k of the comparison  increases  the error decreases at a rate of 1 over  k  however as we had earlier noted  increasing this order  also makes eliciting accurate responses  from the human expert harder  thus by wearing these value of k one can  cover an entire spectrum of this  tradeoff  it's also important to note here that  our proposed algorithm is actually query  efficient  it makes order n log k queries to the k  comparison oracle  and actually any statistically efficient  algorithm  needs to make at least n queries to the  oracle to learn about the utilities on  all the data points  now given this upper bound it's a  natural question to ask  whether this additional error term uh  comprising of 1 over k  and the error of the erm classifier is  indeed necessary  or is it just a manifestation of our  analysis  to answer this we prove a lower bound  theorem which shows that for any k  greater than 1 there exists prediction  problems such that the minimax risk  of any algorithm is lower bounded by a  constant over k  thus we established that our plugin  estimator is  actually statistically minimax optimal  to conclude our artwork formalizes  a class of supervised learning problems  wherein the underlying utility function  is unknown to the learner a priority  we study a natural plug-in estimator and  established that it is statistically  minimax optimal  furthermore in the course of our  analysis we also reveal an interesting  accuracy elicitation trade-off within  this framework  please check out our paper to learn more

